<!--
  A sample CLAUDE.md section for an agent working in a project where this package's hooks are
  deployed. Copy what's useful into your own project's (or user-level) CLAUDE.md — this isn't
  meant to be dropped in verbatim and left untouched; it's a starting point, adapted from the
  actual operating principles this package's own authors run day to day.

  Why this exists at all: the hooks do the mechanical part (inject on prompt, distill on Stop,
  queue for review) without being asked. But good JUDGMENT about what to do with what recall
  hands you, and what's actually worth turning into a durable memory, isn't mechanical — that's
  a discipline an agent has to be told, the same way any other working convention is. This is
  that discipline, written down.
-->

## Working with recall (this project's agent memory)

A `UserPromptSubmit` hook queries your Vectros store automatically and injects relevant hits
before you see the prompt. You do not have to ask for this — it already ran.

- **If something was injected, read it before you start working.** It's there because it matched
  the prompt (or, on the first turn of a session, several derived facets of it) — it's not
  optional color.
- **A hit is authoritative for what was *decided* — not evidence of what the code *currently
  does*.** A memory can tell you a past session settled on an approach, chose a convention, or
  hit a specific gotcha. It cannot tell you the code still works that way today; verify current
  behavior against the code and its tests, the same as you would from a comment or a doc.
- **Check for staleness before trusting a hit at face value** — if the record or doc a hit points
  at carries some kind of status/superseded marker, a superseded item can still rank highly by
  similarity; don't treat "it matched" as "it's still true."
- **If nothing was injected, that is not the same as "nothing relevant exists" — except in an
  excluded project (see the last section), where nothing is injected on purpose and you must not go
  looking.** Recall is a
  best-effort search over a rolling window of the conversation, not an exhaustive one. If you're
  about to make a nontrivial decision and nothing surfaced, consider querying the store yourself
  before assuming there's nothing to find — silence from an automatic pass is a weaker signal
  than a deliberate search that came back empty.
- **A hit that shows up mid-task, related to code you just touched rather than to the original
  ask, is signal — not noise.** The prompt that kicked off a task can miss context that becomes
  obvious once you're actually in the code; a later hit catching that is the mechanism working as
  intended, not a distraction to wave off.

## Deciding what to do with a proposed candidate

When a session accumulates enough new transcript, a background worker distills candidates —
durable facts, decisions, gotchas — and stages them in a review queue. Nothing in that queue is
searchable or written to your knowledge base until you (or the agent, with the right tool access)
explicitly disposes of it via `dispose.mjs`. Settling one well is worth more than settling one
fast. Three questions, asked of every candidate before you settle it — and it's the second and
third that are easy to skip:

1. **Is it actually true?** Verify against the thing that *determines* the behavior — the code,
   the config, the live state — never against a document that merely *describes* it. A doc can be
   stale; the artifact it describes can't lie to you the same way.
2. **Is it already known?** Before storing, check whether this is already covered — by an
   existing memory record, by your repo's own documentation, or by something a reader could
   derive on demand from the code or its history. Storing a near-duplicate doesn't just waste
   space; every stored record is recalled into future sessions' context, so a redundant one taxes
   every session that comes after it for no benefit.
3. **Would a future session actually act differently for having read this?** This is the bar that
   does the real filtering, and it's the one that's easiest to let slide. True-and-novel is not
   sufficient on its own — ask it of a session that never lived through this one: would reading
   this record change a decision, or would the session have landed in the same place anyway? A
   play-by-play of what happened ("fixed X, tests passed"), a fact the code or its own tests
   already answer on demand, or a restatement of something already documented elsewhere all fail
   this test even when they're completely true.

Settle accordingly:

- **Genuinely durable and novel** → `stored:<record-id>` (or point it at documentation you just
  wrote — `documented:path#anchor`). If it's the kind of thing another contributor should be able
  to find in your repo's own conventions doc rather than only through semantic search, write it
  there and dispose the candidate as `documented`, not `stored` — a convention that's discoverable
  by reading the file, not just by getting lucky with a query, serves readers who never asked
  recall the right question.
- **Doesn't clear question 1 or 2** → `ignored:<reason>`, and say *why* — a bare ignore is a black
  box to whoever reads the disposition log later, including a future you.
- **Not sure yet** → leave it pending rather than guess. A wrong `ignored` deletes a candidate that
  won't come back; a wrong `stored` pollutes every future session's recall. Pending costs nothing
  but a line in the queue — a pending candidate keeps getting surfaced to whichever session is
  around, on purpose, so it is never silently forgotten. It doesn't stay pending forever, though:
  if nobody settles it, the orphan-cap backstop (see the README) eventually auto-disposes it
  `ignored` on its own. That backstop is insurance against a candidate rotting in the queue
  unbounded — it is not a substitute for actually reviewing, and it's always reversible with
  `--reopen` if it acted on something that deserved a second look.

## The loop is provisional by design — use that

Recall injects automatically; capture distills automatically; but disposition is a decision, on
purpose. Nothing you capture reaches another session's context until something — you, or an agent
you've trusted with the tool access — has looked at it and said yes. Don't try to route around
that by disposing everything as `stored` reflexively to "clear the queue" — an unreviewed queue is
honest about what it is; a queue full of low-value stored records is not, and it's much harder to
notice and fix later.

